The '''Xiriirka Fotbal Burawi''' ('''XFB'''; in English, the '''Burawi Football Federation''') is the national governing body of [[association football]] in [[Burawa]]. Based in [[Cagaarka Tower]] in [[Samara]], it oversees the [[Burawi football league system]], the [[Burawa national football team]], referee administration, club licensing, youth development, and the operation of the [[Burawi Fotbal Listing Exchange]].

The federation is one of the central sporting institutions in Burawa and is closely associated with the country's socialist-era sports reforms. Its Burawi-language name uses ''fotbal'' rather than an English-derived form, and the abbreviation '''XFB''' is commonly used in Burawi-language references and internal federation documents.

== History ==

=== Founding and early years ===

The XFB was founded in 1955 in [[Samara]], during the early years of the modern Burawi state. It was created to unify regional football administrations, standardise competition rules, and provide Burawa with a single national body capable of affiliating with international football organisations. In its earliest years, the federation concentrated on organising the national team and coordinating the country-wide league structure that would later evolve into the modern four-tier system.

The federation joined the [[Adonian Football Association]] (AFA) the same year it was founded, giving Burawa formal recognition in continental competition. Over the following decades, the XFB became the principal administrator of the domestic game, overseeing match regulations, refereeing standards, and club registration.

=== The 1972 reforms ===

{{Main|1972 Burawi Fotbal Reform Act}}

The federation played a central role in the implementation of the [[1972 Fotbal Reform Act]], which transformed Burawi football into a system built around {{w|workers' cooperative|workers' cooperative}} ownership, {{w|salary cap|salary caps}}, and a national drafting system. The reforms were intended to preserve competitive balance, reduce inequality between clubs, and strengthen player development through a state-supported academy structure.

Under the reforms, the XFB assumed expanded authority over club licensing, youth development, and national competition planning. The federation also helped create the national academy network, including the [[Samara General Fotbal Academy]], which became the flagship institution of the Burawi football development system.

=== Cagaarka Tower headquarters ===

In 1989, the XFB moved its headquarters to [[Cagaarka Tower]] in central [[Samara]]. The building later acquired the informal nickname '''Fotball Tower''' because of the federation's presence and its role as a hub of national football administration. The move concentrated the federation's executive offices, competitions staff, refereeing administration, licensing departments, and national-team office in a single location.

The XFB now occupies much of the building alongside the [[Burawi Fotbal Listing Exchange]], which has been headquartered there since 1997. The shared location has made Cagaarka Tower one of the most important administrative centres in Burawi sport.

== Responsibilities ==

The XFB's responsibilities include:

* overseeing the [[Burawi football league system]]
* registering and licensing clubs
* appointing and training referees
* organising the [[Burawi Cup]] and other national competitions
* coordinating the [[Burawa national football team]]
* administering the national youth and academy system
* setting and enforcing competition regulations
* managing the federation's relationship with the [[Burawi Fotbal Listing Exchange]]

The federation also coordinates disciplinary matters, competition calendars, draft logistics, and compliance with ownership and salary regulations established under the reform era.

== Relationship with the BFLE ==

The XFB established the [[Burawi Fotbal Listing Exchange]] (BFLE) in 1989 to provide a regulated marketplace for club shares. The exchange was created in response to the growth of informal share trading and to make sure the ownership rules introduced by the reform system could be enforced transparently.

Although the BFLE operates as a specialised securities exchange, it remains tightly linked to the federation. The XFB oversees the exchange's regulatory framework, sets the club ownership rules that govern trading, and uses the BFLE as part of the broader system of football administration in Burawa.

== Organisation ==

The federation is organised around several internal departments, including competitions, refereeing, licensing, finance, youth development, and national-team administration. Its leadership structure has traditionally been centralised, reflecting the country's broader tendency toward coordinated public institutions.

The XFB is also responsible for cooperation with regional football administrations and with the public authorities that oversee sport, culture, and infrastructure in Burawa.

